# Irodora — environment contract
#
# ============================================================================
# THIS FILE IS ALMOST EMPTY, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
# ============================================================================
#
# Irodora is a local-first mobile app. There is no server, no database URL, no
# cache, no object store, no identity provider, no mail transport and no
# telemetry endpoint — see ADR-0051. Everything the app needs is on the device.
#
# The 46 variables that used to live here (IRODORA_DATABASE_URL, IRODORA_REDIS_URL,
# IRODORA_BLOB_*, IRODORA_OIDC_*, IRODORA_SESSION_SECRET, IRODORA_KMS_*,
# IRODORA_SMTP_URL, IRODORA_OTEL_*, IRODORA_RATE_LIMIT_*) described the server
# tier. They are readable at the `pre-rehaul-server` tag if one is ever needed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THE FILE STILL EXISTS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The `state` gate enforces a two-way contract: every IRODORA_* variable read
# anywhere in the workspace must be documented here, and this file is where a
# reviewer looks to find out what configuration exists. Deleting the file would
# delete the contract, and the first variable someone adds would arrive
# undocumented with nothing to say so.
#
# So: an empty contract, actively checked, rather than no contract.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT DOES *NOT* BELONG HERE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# - The SQLCipher database key. It is generated on the device at first run and
#   stored in the iOS Keychain / Android Keystore via expo-secure-store. A
#   database key in an environment variable is a database key in a build log.
#
# - A SECRET VALUE. Every variable below is declared here and set NOWHERE in
#   this repository. The build-time ones come from GitHub Actions secrets, which
#   the workflow injects into one job on one ephemeral runner.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CURRENT VARIABLES — all build-time, none read at runtime
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ADR-0058 replaced the EAS assumption this file used to record ("signing
# credentials live in EAS, managed by the store tooling"). Builds are produced
# by GitHub Actions running Gradle, so the credentials are ours to hold, and
# the honest place to say so is here. docs/operations/signing-and-credentials.md
# is the procedure; every step in it is a step only a person can take.

# The version being built, from the git tag. Read by apps/mobile/app.config.ts.
# ABSENT: version 0.0.0, versionCode 1 — an obviously-not-a-release build, which
# is what a hand-run or an on-demand test APK should look like on a phone.
# versionCode is monotonic forever: a code that has been published to a store is
# a code that can never be reused, on any branch.
IRODORA_VERSION_NAME=0.0.0
IRODORA_VERSION_CODE=1

# The Android release keystore, read by the Groovy that
# apps/mobile/plugins/withReleaseSigning.ts injects into the generated project.
#
# ABSENT: the release signing config has no storeFile and AGP FAILS the release
# packaging task. It never falls back to the debug key — a release-labelled
# artefact signed with the template's debug key is installable by anyone,
# attributable to nobody, and can never be updated by a properly signed build.
# Debug builds do not read these at all.
#
# NEVER commit a value. NEVER put the keystore in the repository. In CI the file
# is written to the runner's temp directory from a base64 secret and dies with
# the runner; gate 16 then checks the signer certificate in the APK, so a build
# signed with the wrong key fails before it can be published.
IRODORA_ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PATH=
IRODORA_ANDROID_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD=
IRODORA_ANDROID_KEY_ALIAS=
IRODORA_ANDROID_KEY_PASSWORD=

# OPTIONAL. The keystore format, when it is not what AGP would assume.
# ABSENT: AGP uses KeyStore.getDefaultType(), which is PKCS12 on JDK 9+.
# Set it to PKCS12 for a keystore generated with openssl (the path
# docs/operations/signing-and-credentials.md documents, because this project is
# maintained on a machine with no JDK and therefore no keytool). A wrong guess
# here fails at the very end of a twenty-minute build, so it is worth stating.
IRODORA_ANDROID_KEYSTORE_TYPE=
